WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:16.848
I had become good friends with Jerry's other horse, Captain.

2
00:00:17.929 --> 00:00:22.472
The noble, old war horse was good company, and I never imagined that he would leave us.

3
00:00:23.612 --> 00:00:26.694
And I suppose he wouldn't have, if not for the accident.

4
00:00:27.795 --> 00:00:29.556
I wasn't there, so I only know what I heard.

5
00:00:31.644 --> 00:00:36.166
It seemed that Jerry and Captain had just dropped off a customer when they heard a terrible commotion

6
00:00:36.206 --> 00:00:38.687
nearby. "Clear the way!"

7
00:00:39.147 --> 00:00:39.787
someone yelled.

8
00:00:39.807 --> 00:00:41.468
"Runaway cart!"

9
00:00:42.809 --> 00:00:45.130
Jerry and Captain turned to see something terrible.

10
00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:51.232
A cart pulled by a team of large, panicky draft horses was out of control and racing toward

11
00:00:51.272 --> 00:00:56.874
them! It all happened so fast that Jerry and Captain couldn't move out of the way in time.

12
00:00:59.355 --> 00:01:03.274
The cart crashed into them, knocking over both cab and horse.

13
00:01:04.535 --> 00:01:07.638
Jerry was thrown free and escaped with only bumps and bruises.

14
00:01:08.198 --> 00:01:09.659
Poor Captain wasn't so lucky.

15
00:01:11.161 --> 00:01:15.904
It was a sad sight to see him limp into the stable, sore and bleeding from numerous cuts

16
00:01:15.965 --> 00:01:16.605
and gashes.

17
00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:21.990
Jerry and the vet did all they could for him, and for a while he seemed to be

18
00:01:22.030 --> 00:01:22.570
getting better.

19
00:01:23.691 --> 00:01:28.795
But Captain was a very old horse, and Jerry soon realized he would never be fit for cab

20
00:01:28.835 --> 00:01:29.336
work again.

21
00:01:30.848 --> 00:01:34.351
"He might recover well enough for you to sell him," the vet said.

22
00:01:34.371 --> 00:01:38.954
"He could bring a few pounds at the horse sale from someone who needs a cart horse."

23
00:01:40.195 --> 00:01:41.116
"Absolutely not!"

24
00:01:41.436 --> 00:01:42.437
Jerry said firmly.

25
00:01:42.457 --> 00:01:45.799
"I won't sell a good old friend into hard work and misery.

26
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:49.121
It's much kinder to put him down here at home.

27
00:01:49.842 --> 00:01:51.944
That way I'll know he won't suffer anymore."

28
00:01:53.525 --> 00:01:57.448
The next day Jerry's son, Harry, took me to the blacksmith for some new shoes.

29
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.841
When I returned, Captain was gone.

30
00:02:03.141 --> 00:02:07.042
I missed the old fellow a lot, and I know Jerry and his family did too.

31
00:02:09.223 --> 00:02:13.624
But the cab had been fixed by then, and there was work to do, so Jerry began the

32
00:02:13.664 --> 00:02:14.784
search for a new horse.

33
00:02:16.324 --> 00:02:18.865
He ran into a young man from a nobleman's stables.

34
00:02:20.125 --> 00:02:23.086
That young man told him about a certain horse in his care.

35
00:02:24.487 --> 00:02:29.428
This horse was young and well-bred, but he'd run away with the carriage and caused an accident.

36
00:02:30.956 --> 00:02:35.579
This left him too blemished for a gentleman's stables so the young man had to sell him.

37
00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:40.443
"He's not the typical cab horse," the friend warned Jerry.

38
00:02:40.463 --> 00:02:42.664
"He's young and quite spirited."

39
00:02:42.684 --> 00:02:47.707
"I can deal with high spirits as long as the horse isn't nasty," Jerry said.

40
00:02:47.727 --> 00:02:51.890
"He's not nasty or vicious at all," the young man said.

41
00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:55.633
"In my opinion, the accident wasn't his fault.

42
00:02:56.694 --> 00:03:00.036
It was a cool day, and the horse hadn't had enough exercise that week.

43
00:03:01.068 --> 00:03:04.050
The checkrein was very tight, and the bit rather harsh.

44
00:03:05.551 --> 00:03:07.832
I think the combination made the horse lose his mind."

45
00:03:07.852 --> 00:03:11.394
"That makes sense to me," Jerry said.

46
00:03:11.414 --> 00:03:16.717
"Perhaps all he needs is some fair treatment and an honest day's work to settle him down.

47
00:03:17.358 --> 00:03:18.558
I'll come see him tomorrow."

48
00:03:20.599 --> 00:03:22.621
The next day the horse came home with Jerry.

49
00:03:23.701 --> 00:03:24.842
His name was Hotspur.

50
00:03:25.863 --> 00:03:28.144
He was only five years old and very handsome.

51
00:03:29.372 --> 00:03:31.293
I greeted him as he looked around the stable.

52
00:03:31.933 --> 00:03:34.474
He answered politely, but seemed distracted.

53
00:03:34.494 --> 00:03:37.074
"It can't be true!"

54
00:03:37.434 --> 00:03:37.855
he said.

55
00:03:37.875 --> 00:03:41.496
"Am I really reduced to being a humble cab horse?"

56
00:03:41.516 --> 00:03:44.156
"It's not a bad life," I told him.

57
00:03:44.177 --> 00:03:46.677
"Yes, the work can be hard.

58
00:03:47.377 --> 00:03:50.198
But Jerry and his family are the kindest masters you could have."

59
00:03:51.579 --> 00:03:53.339
Hotspur snorted and didn't answer.

60
00:03:54.420 --> 00:03:59.854
He seemed disgusted, and spent most of that first night moving around so restlessly that I couldn't sleep.

61
00:04:02.695 --> 00:04:07.277
But after five or six hours of pulling the cab the following day, he returned to the stable

62
00:04:07.337 --> 00:04:09.317
seeming much quieter and more sensible.

63
00:04:10.458 --> 00:04:15.080
Jerry spent a lot of time with him that first week, and before long they understood each other

64
00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:16.880
almost as well as Jerry and I did.

65
00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:22.083
"See?" Jerry told his son, Harry, one day.

66
00:04:22.103 --> 00:04:28.021
"With an easy bit in his mouth and plenty of work, our Hotspur will soon be as gentle

67
00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:28.681
as a lamb.

68
00:04:29.761 --> 00:04:31.662
That nobleman's loss is our gain.

69
00:04:32.762 --> 00:04:36.843
Now we have a fine, strong young horse with many good years ahead of him."

70
00:04:38.404 --> 00:04:40.324
Even Hotspur himself seemed to agree.

71
00:04:44.025 --> 00:04:48.726
suppose I could have ended up in a worse place," he said that evening after we'd finished our

72
00:04:48.766 --> 00:04:54.848
grain. "At least here I don't have to wear that horrible checkrein.

73
00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.969
And the bit is much softer than my old one.

74
00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:58.810
That makes up for a lot."

75
00:05:00.371 --> 00:05:01.552
I agreed with that, of course.

76
00:05:03.113 --> 00:05:07.816
Before long Hotspur had settled in so well that it was hard to remember what things were like

77
00:05:07.856 --> 00:05:08.296
without him.
